📝 Day 38: Notes App (Write, Read, Append)

📌 Project Overview

This is my Day 38 Python project, where I built a Notes App that saves notes in a text file called notes.txt.

The program allows users to write new notes, add notes without deleting existing ones, read saved notes with numbers, delete a selected note, and exit the application.

This project builds on Day 36 by introducing three important file modes:

"w" — Write new content and replace existing content.

"a" — Append new content without deleting existing content.

"r" — Read existing content.

It also uses readlines(), enumerate(), and try/except FileNotFoundError to manage notes.

🎯 Features

✅ Write new notes and replace existing notes.

✅ Ask for confirmation before replacing existing notes.

✅ Add notes without removing previous notes.

✅ Read and display notes with numbering.

✅ Delete a note by entering its number.

✅ Prevent blank notes from being saved.

✅ Save notes in notes.txt so they remain after the program closes.

✅ Handle a missing notes file using try/except FileNotFoundError.

✅ Display a message when the notes file is empty.

✅ Keep the menu running until the user chooses Exit.

✅ Display a message when an invalid menu option is selected.


🧠 Understanding the Three File Modes

Think of notes.txt as a notebook on your computer.

File Mode

Meaning

What Happens to Existing Notes?

"w"

Write

Existing content is erased before new content is written.

"a"

Append

Existing content is kept, and new content is added at the end.

"r"

Read

Content is read without changing it.

1. Write Mode ("w")

with open("notes.txt", "w") as file:
    file.write("Start Fresh\n")

Write mode replaces the existing contents of the file.

Important: Opening an existing file in "w" mode erases its old content immediately. That is why this project asks for confirmation before opening the file in this mode.

2. Append Mode ("a")

with open("notes.txt", "a") as file:
    file.write("Buy Milk\n")

Append mode adds a new note at the end of the file without removing the previous notes.

If notes.txt does not exist, Python creates it.

3. Read Mode ("r")

with open("notes.txt", "r") as file:
    notes = file.readlines()

Read mode retrieves the existing notes. If the file does not exist, Python raises a FileNotFoundError.

My program uses try/except to catch this error instead of allowing the program to stop unexpectedly.

⚙️ How the Program Works

1. Write New Notes

The program asks the user to enter a note and checks whether it is blank.

notes = input("Enter your note: ").title()

if len(notes.strip(" ")) == 0:
    print("No notes yet.")
else:
    ask = input("Are you sure it erases all (y/n): ")

    if ask.lower() == "y":
        with open("notes.txt", "w") as file:
            file.write(f"{notes}\n")
        print("New Note added!")

How it works:

Accept a note from the user.

Convert the note to title case using .title().

Check whether the note contains anything other than ordinary spaces.

Ask for confirmation before replacing the file.

If the user enters y, open the file in "w" mode.

Write the new note and a newline character.

Display a confirmation message.

If the user enters n, the existing file is not changed.

2. Add a Note

with open("notes.txt", "a") as file:
    file.write(f"{notes}\n")

Append mode adds the new note at the end of the file.

For example, if the file already contains:

Buy Milk

and the user adds Call Arun, the file becomes:

Buy Milk
Call Arun

The existing note remains unchanged.

3. Read Notes with Numbers

with open("notes.txt", "r") as file:
    notes = file.readlines()

if len(notes) == 0:
    print("No notes yet.")
else:
    for number, note in enumerate(notes, start=1):
        print(f"{number}. {note.strip()}")

Important concepts:

readlines() reads the file into a list of lines.

len(notes) checks how many lines are stored.

enumerate(notes, start=1) gives each note a number beginning at 1.

note.strip() removes surrounding whitespace, including the newline character, for display.

If the file does not exist, the program catches FileNotFoundError. If the file exists but contains no notes, it prints No notes yet..

4. Delete a Note — Bonus Feature

The program displays all notes and asks the user which number to delete.

num = int(input("Enter note number to delete: "))

if 1 <= num <= len(notes):
    removed = notes.pop(num - 1)

    with open("notes.txt", "w") as file:
        file.writelines(notes)

    print(f"Deleted: {removed.strip()}")
else:
    print("Invalid note number.")

Why use num - 1?

Users see note numbers starting at 1, but Python list indexes start at 0.

Note Number

List Index

1

0

2

1

3

2

The program removes the selected note from the list using .pop(), then rewrites the remaining notes to the file using "w" mode.

This works because the program first reads the notes into memory, removes the selected item, and then saves the updated list.

5. Exit the Program

The program uses a while True loop to display the menu repeatedly.

When the user selects option 4, the program prints Bye and uses break to exit the loop.



